WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.826
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.532 --> 00:00:08.740
WILLIAM DENNIS: I'm William
Dennis, a product manager

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.500
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.800
I'm going to cover three
broad topics today.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.010
The first is, why Kubernetes?

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.770
The second is best practices
for application deployment

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.570
on the Kubernetes-- and
lastly, planning for growth.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.290
So let's get started
with, why Kubernetes?

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.500
And I guess it helps to
actually explain what Kubernetes

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.800
for those not familiar.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.060
So people call Kubernetes
a container orchestrator,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.280
which is really just a
fancy way of saying that it

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.290
manages your containers.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.174
The application developer is
responsible for containerizing

00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:42.840
their application and describing
properties of that application,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.150
like the memory and
CPU requirements.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.150
And then an operator is
responsible for deploying

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.430
that application to the
cluster, which could actually

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.990
be the same person.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.289
Kubernetes then takes
that and manages the kind

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:56.330
of heavy lifting for you.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.140
So basically, it
just works out where

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.050
to put those containers--
a very simplified overview.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.870
What makes it popular?

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.760
Well, one of the top reasons
is that you get the language

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.010
flexibility of containers.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.510
So there's no requirement of
what language you're using,

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.970
or what versions the libraries,
or anything like that.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.803
There's less vendor
lock-in with Kubernetes.

00:01:16.803 --> 00:01:19.890
Kubernetes is based
on open source.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.412
And in fact, Google runs
certified Kubernetes.

00:01:22.412 --> 00:01:24.870
This is a version that's been
certified by the Cloud Native

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.540
Computing Foundation
using a certification

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.837
program that, actually, I
participated in creating.

00:01:29.837 --> 00:01:31.920
And there are about 50
other vendors that are also

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.160
offering certified Kubernetes.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.576
So you can be assured that,
whatever you do on Kubernetes,

00:01:35.576 --> 00:01:38.640
you can run it in a number
of different places.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.770
It's also highly
resource optimized.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.110
So you can really pack
a lot of containers

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.290
on the same pool of
computer resources.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.430
You can build your
own pipelines.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.910
So let's say, if you have
a particular opinion how

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.370
you want to deploy software,
you want a blue-green type

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.440
deployment or
whatever preference

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.020
you have for deploying
things, I guarantee

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.630
there's a way you can
do it on Kubernetes.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.150
There are also just tons
of open source add-ons.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.740
So because the project
is open source,

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.032
there's this massive community.

00:02:06.032 --> 00:02:08.031
And you get all these
great open source add-ons.

00:02:08.031 --> 00:02:08.560
You name it.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.925
There's probably an open source
add-on that can help you.

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:13.800
And finally, it lets
you deploy a whole bunch

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.690
of different types
of applications

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:21.150
beyond just a 12-factor
stateless application.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.531
So I mentioned at the outset
that containers is one

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:24.780
of the most important reasons.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.530
Maybe it's worth just
giving a quick recap

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.750
on why containers are so good.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.051
So if we look back at how
you might have deployed

00:02:31.051 --> 00:02:33.300
multiple applications on a
single machine in the past,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.550
it probably would have looked
like the one on the left

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.780
there with a shared machine.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.405
We have a whole bunch
of different apps

00:02:38.405 --> 00:02:40.110
sitting on the one machine.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.790
There was no isolation
between the apps.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.890
So if one of them started
using all the resources,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.370
it would impact all of the apps.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.370
There were common shared
libraries between them.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.786
So it's a real pain if you had
to have a different version

00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:52.247
of the dependencies.

00:02:52.247 --> 00:02:54.330
Then we moved to VMs, where
you got some isolation

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.050
between the apps.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.966
And you had isolation,
in the sense that there

00:02:56.966 --> 00:02:58.020
was no common libraries.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.674
But it was a lot more
expansive and inefficient,

00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:02.340
because the kernel
is getting duplicated

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.714
in each of these VMs.

00:03:03.714 --> 00:03:05.880
Which finally brings us to
containers, which kind of

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.010
gives you the isolation
properties of VMs,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.050
particularly when you add
in things like gVisor,

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.620
some new open source that Google
released a couple of months

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.830
ago.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.510
You get the same
property of the VM

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.720
in that the library
is not shared.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.479
But there's a lot less overhead.

00:03:21.479 --> 00:03:22.270
And we should know.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.311
Because Google's actually
been running containers

00:03:24.311 --> 00:03:26.154
in production for over 12 years.

00:03:26.154 --> 00:03:27.820
And for about three
years prior to that,

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.960
we were heavily developing
this technology,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.780
including contributing some of
the underpinnings like cgroups

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.700
to Linux.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.060
So back to Kubernetes.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:40.320
Kubernetes is a
workload-level abstraction.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.019
And what I mean by that is that
you describe your resources

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:45.060
in terms of the workload.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.400
Like, I want 10 of
container A, and I

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.670
want 20 of container B. I
want them grouped together

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:51.810
on adjacent nodes.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.607
Or I want them spread
far around the world.

00:03:53.607 --> 00:03:56.190
Or maybe something different--
maybe I want to render a movie.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.174
And I want to render that one
frame at a time as a batch job.

00:03:59.174 --> 00:04:01.590
And I want to use the cheapest
compute resource available,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.564
so like the preemptable VM.

00:04:04.564 --> 00:04:05.980
It's a workload-level
abstraction.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.920
So you just define
those workloads.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.160
And its job is to
handle that for you.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.320
I believe it sits at the ideal
level of abstraction, as well.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.960
So it doesn't do a
super low level such

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.316
that you have to manage
individual machines.

00:04:18.316 --> 00:04:20.399
But it's not super high
level either, in the sense

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:22.440
that it doesn't tell you
how to design your apps.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.510
It really sits
nicely in the middle.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.060
To me, this contrasts
with the Platform

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.221
as a Service or the PaaS style,
where delays were a little bit

00:04:30.221 --> 00:04:30.720
blander.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.910
The application and
the PaaS layer that

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.500
sits on top of the IaaS, the
Infrastructure as a Service.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.150
And you can see these, because
the PaaS is actually typically,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.755
like, changing some
code in the application.

00:04:41.755 --> 00:04:43.380
This can be very
convenient, because it

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.680
can do helpful things for you.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.250
But it can also constrain you.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:51.060
Kubernetes, I think, has a
very nice layer separation,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.165
keeping all these
concerns quite separate.

00:04:55.165 --> 00:04:57.040
And the main point about
Kubernetes, I think,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.810
is that it really
prioritizes making

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:03.386
hard tasks possible over
simple tasks easier.

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:05.010
So I guarantee you,
if you have a task,

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.510
and it's already quite easy,
and you want to make it easier,

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.136
you can probably find
a way to do that.

00:05:09.136 --> 00:05:10.551
There's got to be
someone that can

00:05:10.551 --> 00:05:11.890
help you make it even easier.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:12.530
And why not?

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.160
It's always good
to optimize things.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.050
But you have to ask
yourself, I think,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.970
am I actually making
the hard tasks harder

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:21.860
by pursuing this route?

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.520
Kubernetes, people
probably won't

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.022
say that it makes
simple tasks easier.

00:05:26.022 --> 00:05:28.480
I would say it doesn't make
simple tasks particularly hard.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.350
But where it really
excels is that you

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.840
can capture just a
broad range of things

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.620
and do it in a fairly
streamlined way.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.460
And why Kubernetes Engine?

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.070
So this is Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.370
the hosted product
that runs Kubernetes

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.170
that my team manages.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:49.751
It's actually the same team
that writes and contributes

00:05:49.751 --> 00:05:51.250
the majority of the
open source that

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.661
brings you Kubernetes Engine.

00:05:52.661 --> 00:05:54.160
So if you're looking
for people that

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.740
have real expertise
in Kubernetes,

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:58.660
definitely take a look
at our product here.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.870
And maybe more important
than software, I think,

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.312
is the attitude that
we have to open source.

00:06:06.312 --> 00:06:07.770
So this is a quote
from Eric Brewer

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.728
at the keynote last year,
where he said, "We're

00:06:09.728 --> 00:06:11.670
going to be the open cloud.

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.310
We're going to give you the
freedom to join and to leave.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.840
And I'll take my chances."

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.520
And I think this really aligns
us with your objectives,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:21.690
as well.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.450
By making it so easy for
you to come and leave,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.090
we're incentivized to make
it as good as possible

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.920
so that you stay
because it's good,

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:30.190
not because you're locked in.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:35.610
So I think there our objectives
get quite well-aligned.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.080
So before I jump
into a demo, let

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.050
me cover a few key
Kubernetes concepts

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.020
to help explain the demo.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:46.360
I covered containers already.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.080
Pod-- in Kubernetes,
there's the object

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:50.580
called a pod, which
is actually just

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.005
a collection of containers.

00:06:52.005 --> 00:06:55.700
It could be one or many.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.304
This is the smallest
schedulable unit in Kubernetes.

00:06:59.304 --> 00:07:00.720
And the reason we
have a pod is so

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.850
that, if you have
tightly-coupled containers that

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.410
need to be deployed
together, you

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:05.850
can represent them like this.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:09.290
But often, a pod will
just be one container.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:10.400
Then we have nodes.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.841
Nodes are just a way
of saying machines.

00:07:12.841 --> 00:07:14.840
We call it nods, because
they can either be a VM

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.010
or could be bare metal.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.440
As you saw at the keynote
yesterday morning,

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:20.300
GKE now works on prim.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.850
So this will be more
and more common.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.100
And then there's a deployment.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.680
The deployment is the
real driver in Kubernetes.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.840
It's a statement of the
desired number of pods

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.350
that you want to be running.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.980
So in this example, we have
deployment A, which says,

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:37.100
I want two of part A. And we
have deployment B that says,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.150
I want one of my pod.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.530
Kubernetes uses what's called
an operator pattern, where

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.150
you will observe the
state of the cluster.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.399
It will look at what you have
specified, the desired state.

00:07:50.399 --> 00:07:51.940
And it will drive
that observed state

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.860
to the desired state for you.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.490
So in this case, the user
has said, I want two of part

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:01.490
A and on of part B. And that is
what Kubernetes is observing.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.830
But what would happen if
that node became unhealthy?

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.980
Well, Kubernetes would observe
that there's actually now

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.750
only one of pod A none of
pod B. It would redeploy

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:16.880
those pods onto a
node that was fine,

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:18.927
after which the
observed state, again,

00:08:18.927 --> 00:08:20.010
matches the desired state.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.510
And everything is
right in the world.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:24.030
That's deployment.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.450
Last one I want to
cover is service.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:29.370
So a service is really just
a group of identical pods

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.159
that host like an HTTP
service, or a TCP service,

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.840
or whatever you are
trying to provide.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.730
It's just a way of
collecting all the pods

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.549
and addressing them together.

00:08:38.549 --> 00:08:41.190
OK, so with that, let
me talk a little bit now

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:44.640
about how to actually deploy
things into Kubernetes.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.750
And it all starts by dockerizing
and deploying to Kubernetes.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:49.840
Let's switch to the demo.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.230
OK, so I have a very
simple Node.js app here.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.550
And I added a
10-second startup time

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:03.974
for reasons that will
be apparent in a moment.

00:09:03.974 --> 00:09:07.030
It's just to emulate an app that
takes a little while to boot.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.690
Let's take a look at this app.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:13.790
It's extremely simple.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.730
So that's running
not in containers.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.260
It's just running on my machine.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.950
Let's take this app, put it
into Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.866
and put it into a container.

00:09:22.866 --> 00:09:25.240
So I mean, I can build this
locally with the Docker Build

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.160
command.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.640
But actually, the best practice
is to use a Cloud Build tool.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.470
Because you don't know if--

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.320
maybe I had some
change files here

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.310
on my machine that weren't
in version control.

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.685
I probably don't want those
changes going into production

00:09:38.685 --> 00:09:39.910
with code review.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:42.530
So let's not deploy that
container I built locally.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.400
Let's set up a Cloud
Build environment.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.150
To use Cloud Build,
I'll need a source repo.

00:09:51.527 --> 00:09:52.985
I probably should
pick the project.

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:04.431
OK, I'm going to create
the repo called Next Demo.

00:10:06.544 --> 00:10:08.460
By the way, that thing
that just happened that

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.110
was me just tapping
the security key.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:16.920
It just helps keep everything
safe, a little second factor

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.390
device.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:22.540
So a repository that's
created is called Next Demo.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.960
And I'm going to
push the code up.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.380
OK, so while it's getting pushed
up, let me go to Cloud Builder

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:33.275
now.

00:10:33.275 --> 00:10:34.900
And I'm going to
create a build trigger

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.200
that's going to build this
code into a container.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:39.970
So we'll pick Google
Source Repository.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:43.201
You can also connect this
to Bitbucket and GitHub.

00:10:43.201 --> 00:10:47.252
I'm going to pick that
repo that I just created.

00:10:47.252 --> 00:10:49.710
And I'm literally going to
leave everything as the default.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.543
So we want to build
this from a docker file.

00:10:51.543 --> 00:10:52.480
That all looks good.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.791
Let's just create the trigger.

00:10:54.791 --> 00:10:57.040
So normally, this would be
triggered when I push code.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.090
I'm just going to
trigger it manually,

00:10:58.090 --> 00:10:59.256
so you can see what happens.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.420
And we should see-- here we go.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.450
OK, so that container is
now building in the cloud--

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:13.566
very similar to when
I just built it then.

00:11:13.566 --> 00:11:15.060
I'll wait for that to finish.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.226
It only takes a few seconds.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.400
I'm just was running
my npn install there.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.580
This is a little node app.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.150
And we're done.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:31.404
All right, so let's get
these into Kubernetes Engine.

00:11:31.404 --> 00:11:32.820
So I go over to
Kubernetes Engine.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:37.410
And I'm going to pick the myapp
cluster that I created earlier.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.440
And I'm going to click
this Deploy button here.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:44.100
This is kind of the simplest
way to get started, by the way.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:49.570
I'm going to select the
container that I just built.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:50.920
I'm going to give it a name--

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.260
let's call it hellonext--

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.520
and deploy.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.280
And that's basically it.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:11:59.759
It's quite simple.

00:11:59.759 --> 00:12:01.300
There is one more
thing I need to do.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.110
I need to actually expose the
service so that we can actually

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.380
view this online.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:10.030
So because my container
was running port 8080,

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:13.465
I'm going to target
port 8080 from port 80.

00:12:13.465 --> 00:12:15.090
I'm going to pick
the service type load

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:20.750
balancer so that you can all
take a look and click Expose.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.131
So if I now go to the
Services tab there,

00:12:23.131 --> 00:12:25.130
you can see that there's
one service here, which

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:26.800
is creating endpoints.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.754
Since that takes
a minute or so--

00:12:28.754 --> 00:12:30.170
because that IP
address space goes

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.270
to get broadcast to
every point of presence

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.270
that Google has
around the world--

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.400
I'm just going to look at this
one that I created earlier.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.370
And that is the application that
I just demonstrated locally now

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:41.420
running in the cloud--

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.209
pretty straightforward, right?

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:44.375
Let's go back to the slides.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:51.510
All right, but one of the
advantages of Kubernetes

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.840
is that it can really
help you automate

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.452
a whole bunch of different
things in your cluster.

00:12:57.452 --> 00:12:59.160
For all these great
things actually work,

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.160
though, you need to
actually give it some hints,

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.970
and let it know how
it can help you.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.690
One of the most important
things that you need to specify

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.960
are these liveness
and readiness checks.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:13.620
These are probes that run at
frequent intervals checking

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:16.650
the consistency
of the container.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.420
They look very similar, but they
have an important difference.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.600
So the liveness check is
intended to just check

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:22.900
the integrity of the container.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.517
Is the container running?

00:13:24.517 --> 00:13:26.100
Now if the container
actually crashes,

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.030
Kubernetes is smart
enough to restart it.

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:31.140
But if your application
hangs, Kubernetes

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.240
doesn't know if the
application hung

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:34.980
or if it's running just fine.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:36.740
So this is why you
need to have a probe.

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.490
And that's what the
liveness probe is for.

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:41.160
Is my container just operating?

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.080
Readiness is a slightly
different check--

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.661
although it uses
the same function--

00:13:45.661 --> 00:13:47.910
where you indicate to
Kubernetes that the container is

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:49.530
ready to receive traffic.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.030
So it's a slightly
different state

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:54.094
to whether it's
actually running.

00:13:54.094 --> 00:13:56.010
Your container may have
an external dependency

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.150
like on cloud SQL.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.539
In which case it could
be alive and running.

00:14:00.539 --> 00:14:02.580
But it may not have that
database connection yet,

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.040
in which case, it would pass
the liveness check but not

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.440
the readiness check.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.970
Perhaps, a diagram would help.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:13.560
So we have the liveness probe
that goes just from the control

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.550
plane to the container
and a readiness

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.060
probe that goes to the container
and the external dependencies.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:22.070
The reason why you don't
want to mix these two

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:25.460
is that Kubernetes will
actually reboot the container

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:27.897
if it doesn't respond
to a liveness probe.

00:14:27.897 --> 00:14:29.480
So if your liveness
probe is dependent

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.537
on an external service
which goes down,

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:33.620
you don't actually necessarily
want your container

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:34.760
to restart at that moment.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.080
That's why we separate it.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.901
Of course, maybe you
do after a while.

00:14:38.901 --> 00:14:40.400
Because maybe there's
something kind

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:41.820
of funky with that connection.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.610
So you can configure
the readiness probe

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:46.560
to still restart your container
but typically at a slower pace

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:50.480
so that you don't
churn the containers.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.430
There are three options
for these probes.

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.710
One is a command
where Kubernetes

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:57.620
will use the exit status.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:59.790
Then second is a TCP
Port where it will just

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.140
try to open a socket.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.020
If the socket opens,
then everything's good.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:05.830
And the third one
is an HTTP request

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.950
where it will make
an HTTP request

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:10.140
and look at the
HTTP response code.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.726
It's the third one that
I'm going to demo today.

00:15:12.726 --> 00:15:14.100
So let's switch
back to the demo.

00:15:16.908 --> 00:15:18.571
All right, and first
up, I'm actually

00:15:18.571 --> 00:15:21.070
going to show you what happens
if you don't have a readiness

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:22.927
check.

00:15:22.927 --> 00:15:25.510
So to do that, I'm just going
to have a little while loop here

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.520
that's just going
to go, while true,

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.872
I'm going to curl that
endpoint that I just created.

00:15:30.872 --> 00:15:32.830
So you can see, it's just
printing out this app

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.810
that I created and
the current time so

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:37.226
that you can see that there's
actually something changing.

00:15:41.805 --> 00:15:43.430
All right, let's add
something to this.

00:15:50.617 --> 00:15:52.950
All right, I'm going just add
a little smiley face here.

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.990
So it's just some
change that we can demo.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:04.707
All right, and we're
going to push this up.

00:16:04.707 --> 00:16:06.290
And as you know
already, this is going

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:09.470
to build the container for me.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:11.800
So I'm going to
deploy that container.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:15.630
But before I do, just to make
the demo run a little better,

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:18.760
I'm going to take a look
at my deployment here.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:22.470
And I'm just going to remove
that little thing there.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.894
The app that I deployed actually
has an automatic horizontal

00:16:30.894 --> 00:16:33.060
port [INAUDIBLE],, which I
don't want for this demo.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:36.630
But I will be showing
you later, so fear not.

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:39.900
OK, so now that
we're ready to go,

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:43.920
let's see if that container was
built. It looks like it was.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.110
I'll grab the new
container name,

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:48.124
go back to Kubernetes Engine.

00:16:48.124 --> 00:16:50.290
And this is also showing
you how to update your app.

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:52.620
So I'm just going to do
a rolling update here.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:56.187
I'm going to paste in the new
contained image, quick Update.

00:16:56.187 --> 00:16:58.020
And let's switch over,
and see what happens.

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:07.920
So you notice, there's a few
errors on the top right here.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.520
Those errors are happening,
because as I mentioned,

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:14.420
this particular container
has a 10-second boot time.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:18.010
So while it was booting, because
we didn't give it a readiness

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:19.730
check, all the
containers were receiving

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.710
traffic, even the ones
that weren't ready.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:26.310
That's why you see a
bunch of errors there.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:30.920
So let's get back to here,
and add a readiness check.

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:42.210
So this is what the
readiness check looks like.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:45.780
I'll explain what it
all means in a second.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.340
So below the container
in a configuration,

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.027
we're adding a readiness probe.

00:17:50.027 --> 00:17:51.860
We're saying the failure
threshold is three.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:53.660
So if this probe
fails three times,

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.034
I assume that there's a problem,
that the container needs

00:17:56.034 --> 00:17:56.900
to be restarted.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:18:00.640
We'll do a HTTP GET request
with an initial start

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:02.360
up delay of three seconds.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:04.190
We'll run that
every three seconds.

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:05.689
And if it succeeds
once, we're going

00:18:05.689 --> 00:18:07.650
to count that as a success.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.130
So let's save that.

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:11.630
I'm just going to
force update that.

00:18:20.731 --> 00:18:23.260
It looks like I've got a
few terminating pods here

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:25.060
that are taking a
little bit of time.

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:29.010
So let me update the app
while we wait for that.

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.625
And I can demo what the
rollout looks like when

00:18:31.625 --> 00:18:32.750
you have a readiness check.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:51.440
OK, we go back to
Cloud Builder--

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:55.340
same process.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:18:59.950
I think we're in
a good state now.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:03.580
So let's roll this out.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.600
And as we do, let's
observe what happens.

00:19:07.679 --> 00:19:09.720
Oh, by the way, we're
still waiting for the image

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.629
to be built. So that's actually
building in the background.

00:19:12.629 --> 00:19:13.920
I was a little bit quick there.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.090
One of the good things
about Kubernetes

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.160
is-- you see where it
says image pull back off?

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:19.076
It's actually smart
enough not to try and boot

00:19:19.076 --> 00:19:20.650
the content that
doesn't exist yet.

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:21.830
So it's actually
going to keep retrying

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:23.250
that, which is pretty neat.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.527
And it just got it.

00:19:25.527 --> 00:19:27.360
And if you observe here
where it says ready,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:29.360
you can notice that it's
actually not ready yet.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:32.830
And we're still routing traffic
to the previous container.

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:34.800
Once that flips over
to ready, now you

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:36.960
can see that it's
flipping between the two

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.780
different expressions there.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:42.210
Both containers are
receiving traffic.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.660
Then it's going to terminate
the old container, and route

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:47.260
all the traffic to that one.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:50.580
So unlike previously, when
there was an error state

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:53.910
as traffic got routed to
containers that weren't ready,

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:56.197
once you have the
readiness check,

00:19:56.197 --> 00:19:57.280
that error doesn't happen.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:59.550
And you have a
very smooth update.

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:03.057
That's what makes readiness
checks so important.

00:20:03.057 --> 00:20:04.640
All right, back to
the slides, please.

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:11.825
All right, now you may have seen
that I was using a lot of UI

00:20:11.825 --> 00:20:12.325
there.

00:20:12.325 --> 00:20:14.710
And I kind of just
patching the configuration

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:16.784
fairly willy-nilly.

00:20:16.784 --> 00:20:18.700
Maybe it doesn't surprise
you that that is not

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:20.420
the production best practice.

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:24.200
So let's talk a little
bit about what is.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:27.850
So as I've covered, Kubernetes
is build on a declarative style

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:30.610
architecture, where you declare
the state that you want.

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:33.220
And it drives that
observed state to the state

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:34.204
that you declared.

00:20:34.204 --> 00:20:35.620
What this means
is that you end up

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:38.567
with a lot of different
configuration files.

00:20:38.567 --> 00:20:40.150
The workloads and
the services are all

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.720
specified with configuration.

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.761
So what do you do with
all that configuration?

00:20:43.761 --> 00:20:45.760
Well, at Google, we've
actually had this problem

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:46.640
for quite some time.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:48.160
So we have a fairly
good experience

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:49.570
on how to deal with this.

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:52.840
This is a quote from the Google
SRE, the "Site Reliability

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:56.290
Engineering" book, which you
can read online for free.

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:59.800
And it says that, "At Google,
all configuration schemes

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.260
that we use for
storing this config

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:03.790
involve storing
that configuration

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:05.950
in our primary source
code repository

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:09.040
and enforcing a strict
code review requirement."

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:11.050
That is the same code
review requirement

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:14.526
for configuration as for code.

00:21:14.526 --> 00:21:17.150
The benefits of this model-- so
if you're storing configuration

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.110
in Git along with the code
on a separate Git repo--

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:24.600
is you get all the benefits
that you get with storing code

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:26.310
in version control.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:27.510
You get rollbacks.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.464
So if you wrote out
the wrong release,

00:21:30.464 --> 00:21:32.130
you can just rollback
the configuration.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:34.890
And that will effectively
rollback that release.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:36.500
You get versioning
and auditability.

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:39.120
If someone asks you, hey,
what version of our code

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.382
was running at
9:00 AM on Monday?

00:21:41.382 --> 00:21:43.090
Well, if you're just
patching it by hand,

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:44.590
you probably don't know.

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:46.630
But if everything's
stored in Git,

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:48.820
you can just look back
through the commit history

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:52.340
and see what was running
at exactly that time.

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:54.275
You can also utilize
code reviews.

00:21:54.275 --> 00:21:55.900
So you can try and
avoid that situation

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.550
when someone fat fingers the
replica count down to one

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.340
by accident and takes
down the servers.

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:05.100
Disaster recovery-- so while
this is not the primary motive

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:08.360
in making your Kubernetes
application highly available--

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.670
on Kubernetes, we support
regional clusters and a whole

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.040
bunch of different things--

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:14.373
it is, of course, good to
have that configuration stored

00:22:14.373 --> 00:22:16.550
in version control as a backup.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:18.950
And finally, whatever
identity and authorization

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:21.080
model you have on your
code, you can apply that

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:22.560
to the configuration, as well.

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:23.934
So you could
potentially restrict

00:22:23.934 --> 00:22:28.580
it to and SRE-type
role if you wanted to.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:32.680
So what does that look like
in a Git Kubernetes world?

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:34.930
This is just one way to do it.

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:37.607
But it's kind of the
one I recommend--

00:22:37.607 --> 00:22:39.190
where you would have
one configuration

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:40.955
repository per app.

00:22:40.955 --> 00:22:43.330
So in this example, there are
two repos at the top there,

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:46.210
one for the app, Foo ,
one for the app, Bar.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:49.220
And then you use branches for
each environment of the app.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.450
So the master branch
likely maps to production.

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:54.640
You could have a staging branch.

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:57.600
And these branches of
configuration map 1 to 1

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.406
to name spaces in Kubernetes.

00:22:59.406 --> 00:23:01.030
The advantage of that
is that you don't

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.600
have to change the object name.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:04.749
If you have an app
called Foo Deployment,

00:23:04.749 --> 00:23:06.790
you don't have to call it
Foo Deployment Staging.

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:08.290
You can just call
it Foo Deployment,

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:11.227
because you're going to
deploy into a namespace.

00:23:11.227 --> 00:23:12.810
It's up to you if
you want to run this

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:13.980
on two clusters or one.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:15.730
So I've just shown
here, just for example,

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:17.920
say, having two different
clusters and a bunch

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:18.961
of different namespaces.

00:23:18.961 --> 00:23:20.210
That part's totally up to you.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:24.550
How do you get from
the state, though,

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:28.870
that I was demoing into this new
more production grade system?

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:30.870
Well, fortunately
it's pretty easy.

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:33.310
You can just extract all
the configuration resources

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:35.590
that you had so far
using this command,

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:40.732
kubectl get resource
dash o yaml.

00:23:40.732 --> 00:23:42.190
Once you've done
that, you can also

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:45.740
set up some very nice things
like continuous deployment.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.800
So we have a
product, Cloud Build.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:50.590
I demoed it just trying
building the container.

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:54.040
But you can actually use it
to roll out the config change,

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:55.120
as well.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.430
And all you need is this
very simple configuration

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:01.094
file that basically says, I
want to use the kubectl task.

00:24:01.094 --> 00:24:03.010
I want to run the command
at the bottom, which

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:05.110
is apply dash f dot,
which just means

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:08.119
apply the current
directory of config.

00:24:08.119 --> 00:24:09.910
And I'll give it two
environment variables,

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:12.559
so it knows where to run that.

00:24:12.559 --> 00:24:13.600
Let's switch to the demo.

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:15.730
And I'll show you
how I can migrate

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:18.371
this app that I've created
into that new environment.

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.720
First, let's look at
the deployment objects.

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:27.460
We have one deployment
object, hellonext,

00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:29.035
that I created in the UI.

00:24:29.035 --> 00:24:31.010
Now I'm going to download
the config for that.

00:24:35.887 --> 00:24:37.970
I'm going to use the export
command here, as well.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:39.809
So that it kind of
skips some extra config

00:24:39.809 --> 00:24:40.850
that I don't really need.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:49.880
OK, and that's
what it looks like.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:51.690
If you saw briefly
before, that's

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.230
the same config that I was
editing on the website.

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:57.480
I mentioned that we can hook
this up to Cloud Builder.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.670
So let's take a look
at how that will work.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:02.410
To use Cloud
Builder, once again,

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.812
I'm going to create
another source repository.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:10.100
This one, I'll call
Next Demo Config.

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:22.930
I'll go to Cloud Build.

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:26.050
And again, go to
the build triggers.

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:30.790
I'll add a new trigger again
on the Google Cloud Source

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:32.560
repository.

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:34.804
We'll pick here the config 1.

00:25:34.804 --> 00:25:37.220
But instead of using the docker
file, what I'm going to do

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:40.030
is I'm going to point this
at the Cloud Build config

00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:41.930
file, which is also in
this directory, which

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:43.850
I can show you.

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:47.900
That's that same file that
I had up on the slide.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.650
It's in a subdirectory.

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:52.309
So that's all, one change.

00:25:52.309 --> 00:25:54.600
We're just going to point
this at the Cloud Build file.

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:56.475
And we're going to create
that trigger, which

00:25:56.475 --> 00:25:59.110
will sit alongside the code
container building tree

00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:01.400
that I have.

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:06.480
All right, so let's get this in.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.380
Push that up.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:17.302
And we'll take a look at what
happens in the build history.

00:26:17.302 --> 00:26:19.260
So you can see that, just
like we were building

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:21.630
the container before, we're
now actually executing

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:25.370
this config build.

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:27.020
Instead of building
a container, it's

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:30.870
actually just running
this command, which

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.036
is kubectl apply dash f dot.

00:26:33.036 --> 00:26:34.410
Basically, it's
just kind of roll

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:39.260
out all that config in that
Git repo onto the cluster.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:43.495
So let's demo an app update
using continuous deployment.

00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:50.690
Change that one more time.

00:27:03.832 --> 00:27:05.290
And this time, I'm
not going to use

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:08.131
the UI, because I
know what the image is

00:27:08.131 --> 00:27:10.630
going to be named based on the
SHA Let me just look at that.

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:18.450
And here in my config,
I'm going to open up.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:24.547
And I'm going to edit
here the container image.

00:27:24.547 --> 00:27:26.130
I'm just going to
pasting the new SHA,

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:28.845
because I know that Container
Builder is going to create

00:27:28.845 --> 00:27:30.504
a container with that name.

00:27:30.504 --> 00:27:32.420
So I don't have to go
back to the UI any more.

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:45.330
All right, this time, let's
just observe what happens.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:48.520
So in theory, [INAUDIBLE]
in a few seconds,

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:52.130
pick up that commit
automatically, and start

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:53.450
rolling out into the cluster.

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:55.880
Let's get my little curl
while loop back up running.

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:02.510
Yes, there we go.

00:28:02.510 --> 00:28:06.320
So Google Cloud Build
picked up the change,

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:08.240
has applied the change.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:12.239
We're once again waiting for
that readiness check to pass.

00:28:12.239 --> 00:28:13.780
Very soon, on the
right, you're going

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:16.180
to see that change I just
might get rolled out.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:18.040
We're splitting the traffic.

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:22.080
We're going to terminate the
old container in just a moment.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:26.770
So that is kind of the start
of a proper production-grade

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:29.390
pipeline on GKE.

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:32.840
Obviously, you can take
this a lot further.

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:35.250
The team also has various
different ways of doing this.

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:37.040
We have a product
called Spinnaker,

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:38.550
which is fairly advanced.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.780
And we have some partners
in this space like GitLab,

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:45.710
Weaveworks, Codefresh.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:48.560
But that's just
one way to do it.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:49.820
Back to the slides, please.

00:28:53.699 --> 00:28:55.240
So of course, it
goes without saying,

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:56.614
I think-- but I'll
say it anyway.

00:28:56.614 --> 00:28:58.970
Once you moved to
Configuration as Code,

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:00.930
don't go editing
the configuration

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:02.940
or replying it outside
of version control.

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.310
So don't be doing what I was
doing before in the UI once

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:06.607
your moving the system.

00:29:06.607 --> 00:29:08.940
Because then, of course,
you're violating the constraint

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:10.731
that everything went
through a code review.

00:29:12.934 --> 00:29:14.100
OK, so what have we covered?

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.824
We've covered how to
configure your cluster so

00:29:16.824 --> 00:29:19.240
that communities Kubernetes
knows when your application is

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:20.887
running, when it's ready.

00:29:20.887 --> 00:29:23.470
We've talked about how to export
that configuration and get it

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:27.479
into a close-to-production-grade
pipeline.

00:29:27.479 --> 00:29:29.020
There's a couple
more things that you

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.820
need to do to help
Kubernetes help you.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:34.120
And one of those is
telling it what resources

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:37.580
you need for your container.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:40.940
So this comes in two forms,
kind of like the probes before.

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:44.870
But again, they have two
very specific requirements.

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:49.330
The requests is how you
reserve resources for your pod.

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:53.840
And limits constrain how many
resources your pod can use.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:56.440
So if you request all the
resources in the cluster,

00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:00.600
then the schedule will actually
stop scheduling new containers.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.580
Whereas, limits just
restrain your container.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:07.240
I think a diagram is
probably more helpful here.

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:10.720
This is showing one VM
and all of the resource

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:11.860
allocation of that VM.

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:13.870
We have container
A that requested

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:19.270
20% of one CPU, container B that
requested 50%, container C that

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.340
requested 20%.

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:23.580
And then let's look at
what's actually being used.

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:25.950
So that was just the
resource allocation.

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:30.177
But it doesn't actually restrain
what the container can use.

00:30:30.177 --> 00:30:32.010
So here we have container
C using a lot more

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:33.440
than it said it would.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:35.970
We have container B using
a bit less than it said

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:38.252
and A using a bit less, as well.

00:30:38.252 --> 00:30:40.710
is actually really good, because
this means that you're not

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.240
wasting those resources.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:45.210
Even though they were
allocated to container B,

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.900
container C can use them as
long as container B isn't.

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:49.650
So it's kind of like
a way of guaranteeing

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:52.740
a minimum amount of resources
without actually constraining

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:54.510
the maximum.

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:56.580
Now if you do want to
constrain the maximum,

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:58.210
then you can set
a limit, as well.

00:30:58.210 --> 00:30:59.870
So I could have
limited container C

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:03.810
at 25%, in which case, it
won't go over that amount.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:06.390
What this means, though, is
that, if you have a container--

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:08.040
like here, container D--

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:10.380
that is requesting
20%, it's actually not

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.000
going to get scheduled.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:15.840
So even though the CPU does
have 20% capacity available,

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:17.520
because it wasn't
allocated, it's

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:18.807
not going to get scheduled.

00:31:21.797 --> 00:31:23.630
This is how you set it
up in the deployment.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:25.180
So you just put
this in pretty much

00:31:25.180 --> 00:31:28.060
exactly what I did
with the probes.

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.340
I'm actually going
to demo that one.

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:32.077
But it's fairly easy to set up.

00:31:32.077 --> 00:31:33.660
And you can look at
Google Stackdriver

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:37.430
to see what resources your pods
are currently using in order

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:40.600
to get good values for this.

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:42.370
Obviously, you want
to put the request

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:45.250
somewhere above the
minimum that you need.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.920
But obviously, don't
make it excessive.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.180
Don't make it too high.

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:50.830
Because you also kind of
want to catch cases where

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:52.060
you might have a memory leak.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:53.768
That's where the limit
can come in handy.

00:31:53.768 --> 00:31:56.040
And you just limit the
container at certain amount

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:57.790
so that Kubernetes is
restarted if it ever

00:31:57.790 --> 00:31:58.665
uses too much memory.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:04.700
Another thing to consider when
you set limits and the requests

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:06.860
on the container
is how many forked

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:09.900
processes you want actually
inside that container.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:14.270
So this obviously affects
the memory and the CPU usage.

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:15.819
Before Kubernetes,
before containers,

00:32:15.819 --> 00:32:17.360
it was generally
pretty good practice

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:20.075
to have a lot of concurrency
in each version of your app.

00:32:20.075 --> 00:32:21.950
Because it was such a
pain to deploy the app,

00:32:21.950 --> 00:32:24.290
you wanted to make that
app highly concurrent.

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:26.330
But in Kubernetes,
it's very easy

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:28.557
to create multiple
replicas of that app such

00:32:28.557 --> 00:32:30.890
that you don't have to have
a huge amount of concurrency

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:32.090
within the app.

00:32:32.090 --> 00:32:34.852
So what's the right
balance, though?

00:32:34.852 --> 00:32:36.560
I tend to think about
two or three forked

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.177
processes per container
as about the right amount.

00:32:39.177 --> 00:32:40.760
Because you do get
some memory savings

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:43.670
by just forking a process
and making it internally

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:46.660
a little bit concurrent.

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:48.550
But the advantage of
having so many replicas

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:51.070
is that, if one of them
were to crash, then

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:54.040
you haven't removed
all the capacity.

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:57.070
Whereas, if you had only two
replicas and a huge amount

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:59.860
of concurrency inside them,
one of them going away

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:02.550
would be quite problematic.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:05.670
Kind of the same
trade-off for machines--

00:33:05.670 --> 00:33:09.210
so large machines do offer a
slightly greater efficiency

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:10.290
with Kubernetes.

00:33:10.290 --> 00:33:11.730
You can pack more containers in.

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:13.095
There's less wastage.

00:33:13.095 --> 00:33:15.470
But of course, more machines
gives you more availability.

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:18.090
So kind of the same as before,
with more replicas giving you

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:19.440
more availability.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:22.740
So again, just balance the
two trade-offs of machine size

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.540
and machine count to get your
desired availability goal.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:31.170
And what about rapid iteration?

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:34.620
So one of the nice things
about not using containers

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:38.517
is that I could just go Node
server.js whenever I wanted,

00:33:38.517 --> 00:33:39.600
bring up that application.

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:41.960
It was really straightforward.

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:43.760
So for Kubernetes,
we have a tool

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:45.740
called Scaffold that
aims to give you

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:47.450
that kind of quick
iteration that you're

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:50.960
used to while still
using Kubernetes.

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:55.881
Scaffold observes your
directory of configuration code.

00:33:55.881 --> 00:33:57.380
And whenever something
changes, it's

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:59.130
going to redeploy your
app too Kubernetes.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:02.710
So you can very quickly see
any changes you make deployed.

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:06.720
Right.

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:09.170
So that was about
some best practices

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:12.440
of how to describe your
application to Kubernetes

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:14.780
in a way that it can
help you kind of keep

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:16.070
everything running for you.

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:18.380
It can restart the container
if there's a problem.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:20.817
It can split the
traffic accordingly.

00:34:20.817 --> 00:34:21.900
What about growth, though?

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:23.730
What about planning for growth?

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.210
So this is something where
Kubernetes Engine really

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:28.909
excels, actually.

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:34.610
We have three world-class
automation tools

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:36.440
that we offer as part
of Kubernetes Engine.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:38.616
The first is cluster
auto-scaling.

00:34:38.616 --> 00:34:40.699
So when you schedule a
container and there are not

00:34:40.699 --> 00:34:42.949
enough resources, the
Cluster Auto-Scaler

00:34:42.949 --> 00:34:45.980
can automatically
provision more resources

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:48.440
to handle those extra replicas.

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:50.690
But perhaps as
importantly, it can also

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:52.885
remove resources that
are not being used.

00:34:52.885 --> 00:34:54.260
Here at Google,
we don't want you

00:34:54.260 --> 00:34:57.240
to pay for compute resources
that you don't need.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:00.200
So we'll actually help you
scale it back in the quiet times

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:02.220
to save money.

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:04.010
The second one is
Node Auto Repair.

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:06.920
So occasionally, VMs
can become unhealthy.

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:09.860
Node Order Repair is
constantly watching the VMs,

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:12.440
just as Kubernetes is
watching your containers.

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:15.650
And it's going to replace
any VMs that have a problem.

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:17.750
The last one is
Node Auto Upgrade.

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:20.840
So as you've probably seen
from the last couple of years

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:23.060
of news, there's a
lot of vulnerabilities

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:25.409
that the world
discovers in releases.

00:35:25.409 --> 00:35:27.200
And you probably don't
want to stay up late

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:28.700
at night worrying about this.

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:30.567
So if you turn on
Note Auto Upgrade,

00:35:30.567 --> 00:35:31.650
we'll keep you up to date.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:33.760
And we'll apply all these
patches to Kubernetes

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:36.050
right onto the nodes for you.

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:37.820
And these three
features are actually,

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:40.278
to the best of my knowledge,
not available in other clouds.

00:35:40.278 --> 00:35:42.980
These are very much specific
to Google and what makes,

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:45.650
I think, Kubernetes Engine
just such an amazing product.

00:35:48.042 --> 00:35:49.750
To really take advantage
of these things,

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:53.140
though, you actually also need
to auto scale the workload.

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:55.240
So you need some
way of detecting

00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:58.970
when there are more users
coming than what you can serve

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:00.620
and automatically
adding replicas

00:36:00.620 --> 00:36:03.300
of your pod to handle them.

00:36:03.300 --> 00:36:04.800
For that, what we
recommend is using

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:07.080
requests per second metrics.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:10.090
Again, this is kind of like
a best-in-class thing to do.

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:13.590
You can use CPU and
memory usage as a proxy

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:15.200
to scale up your container.

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:17.450
So for example, you could
say, if my container

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:20.970
is running really hot and
is using 90% of the CPU,

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:22.380
add another one.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:24.900
But not every
application is CPU bound.

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:26.670
In fact, that's fairly uncommon.

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:28.350
It's often, there's
a queue length.

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:32.710
There's often a request per
second that the app can handle.

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:35.320
So on Kubernetes Engine, we
make this actually fairly easy

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:38.230
for you to set up.

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:40.780
In conjunction with
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:45.370
we have a Kubernetes Horizontal
Pod Auto-Scaler component that

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:47.410
can read the requests
per second metric

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:51.340
from Stackdriver that's written
to Stackdriver from the HTTP

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:55.950
load balancer, such that,
when new users arrive,

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.950
we can automatically
provision more containers

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:01.080
to handle the increase
in requests per second

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:03.400
from these users.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.540
So what you do is you measure
how many requests per second

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.850
can your application
handle, just

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.670
as you measured how much
RAM and how CPU it used.

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:13.890
It's fairly easy to
measure how many requests--

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.114
what the throughput is
of your application.

00:37:16.114 --> 00:37:18.030
And then you can just
say to Kubernetes, look,

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:21.870
I want each of the replicas
to have a maximum of, say,

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:26.940
six requests per second,
or 100, or whatever it is.

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.570
Setting up a horizontal
pod auto-scaler

00:37:28.570 --> 00:37:30.970
it is really straightforward.

00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:32.700
You just have one
more piece of config.

00:37:32.700 --> 00:37:35.410
I know, Kubernetes is
all that the config.

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.410
There's really two lines
that matter there, too.

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:39.300
It's the ones I've
highlighted in blue.

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:41.850
And that is, my metric
is the request count

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:43.340
coming from the web answer.

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:45.100
And my target value
per container,

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:46.860
in this case, for
this example, is going

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:49.950
to be six requests per second.

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:52.290
I want to have one container
running for every six

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.334
requests per second.

00:37:54.334 --> 00:37:55.500
Let's switch over to a demo.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:56.708
And I'll show that in action.

00:38:00.740 --> 00:38:02.710
All right, so the
bottom right there,

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:06.280
I have Stackdriver monitoring
showing the request per second

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:08.440
on the service.

00:38:08.440 --> 00:38:11.570
And you can see there's just
under two requests per second

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:12.980
happening right now.

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:14.660
Let me add a couple
of while loops

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:16.340
to generate some load on this.

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:24.120
That should be about
20 RPS, I believe.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:28.200
After about 30
seconds to 60 seconds,

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:30.480
that RPS should show
up in Stackdriver.

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:32.812
And we'll see if the
cluster reacts to handling.

00:38:32.812 --> 00:38:34.270
I'm going to set
some watches here.

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:35.978
So we're going to
watch how many replicas

00:38:35.978 --> 00:38:38.880
we have in that container,
how many nodes we have.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:42.690
And for good measure,
I can also watch

00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:47.680
the status of my
horizontal pod auto-scaler.

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:49.476
Now as this takes a
few seconds to show up,

00:38:49.476 --> 00:38:51.100
I'm actually going
to switch to a video

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:53.399
and show you a
30-minute window of what

00:38:53.399 --> 00:38:54.940
happens when a whole
bunch of traffic

00:38:54.940 --> 00:38:57.430
arrives on this
example application.

00:38:57.430 --> 00:39:02.020
But I'll switch back, so you
know I wasn't cheating later.

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:04.470
All right, so this is the
video I recorded on Monday--

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:07.380
exact same demo.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:10.620
And here we've just
added an extra five RPS.

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:14.360
And I'm just going to speed
up the video a little bit.

00:39:14.360 --> 00:39:16.790
So you can see it in the
chart on the bottom right

00:39:16.790 --> 00:39:20.540
that the QPS is just
about to arrive.

00:39:20.540 --> 00:39:23.160
We should clock up there
to about eight or nine.

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:32.560
An because I set a target
of six RPS per container--

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:34.510
it looks like we're
currently at about seven--

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:36.510
you can actually see that
Kubernetes has already

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:39.000
scheduled a second container
at the top left there

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:42.410
to handle that extra traffic.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:46.580
I'm going to speed
up this demo again.

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:49.270
And let's try adding
another 10 QPS of traffic.

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:03.830
And you can see that the 10
QPS registered in Stackdriver.

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:06.830
The Horizontal Pod
Auto-Scaler successfully

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:08.600
scheduled two more containers.

00:40:08.600 --> 00:40:10.520
But it looks like
there's a problem.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.550
So those containers are pending.

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:14.400
They're not running yet.

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:16.640
And the reason is is that
this cluster is actually

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:17.940
out of resources.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:20.750
So those containers are actually
stuck in a pending state.

00:40:20.750 --> 00:40:23.300
Well, the good news
is, as I mentioned,

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:26.670
Kubernetes has a cluster
auto-scaler, as well.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:30.544
So what's going to happen
is GKE, Kubernetes Engine,

00:40:30.544 --> 00:40:32.960
is watching for this state and
has realized that there are

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:35.630
pending contains here that they
don't have the capacity to run.

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:39.810
And so it's actually
automatically

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:44.190
provisioned one more node there,
you can see, in the second

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:47.380
from the top left.

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:50.920
Once that node becomes ready--
and let me just play the demo--

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:52.675
those pending
containers get created.

00:40:55.190 --> 00:40:58.011
And they're now running
and serving traffic.

00:40:58.011 --> 00:41:02.370
All right, so for a bit of fun,
let me just add another 20 QPS.

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:04.110
And we'll speed up
the demo quite fast.

00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:06.880
And we'll see what happens.

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:08.930
Once again, it schedules
some more contentious.

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:10.420
It scheduled some more nodes.

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:14.175
I think about five nodes now.

00:41:14.175 --> 00:41:16.800
But I told you that you're going
to save money, as well, right?

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:21.112
So here I've seen that I did a
whole bunch of users arriving.

00:41:21.112 --> 00:41:22.320
You might have been sleeping.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:23.653
I don't know what was happening.

00:41:23.653 --> 00:41:24.710
It could be the weekend.

00:41:24.710 --> 00:41:27.870
A whole bunch of users arrived
and were successfully served

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:28.660
by your app.

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:29.868
So they weren't disappointed.

00:41:29.868 --> 00:41:32.770
They didn't have a fail
character on the screen.

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:35.460
They were successfully searched
while you were sleeping,

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:38.507
or on vacation, or whatever.

00:41:38.507 --> 00:41:39.840
But what about saving you money?

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.890
All right, if I drop the
load all the way off here,

00:41:42.890 --> 00:41:45.390
drop it back to five QPS.

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:46.870
Let's see what happens.

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:48.870
And I'm going to run the
demo a little bit fast.

00:41:48.870 --> 00:41:51.289
Because as with a lot
of auto-scaling things,

00:41:51.289 --> 00:41:52.830
the scaling down is
a little bit more

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:55.050
conservative than scaling up.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:56.580
The reason that
it's typically set

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.830
that way is that you don't
want to quickly deprovision

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:03.270
resources in case the users
come back straightaway.

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:06.360
So it looks like we've
already deprovisioned the pods

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:07.200
in the top left.

00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:08.297
They're no longer running.

00:42:08.297 --> 00:42:10.380
But we still have some
nodes that are running now.

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:12.213
They're probably not
needed now that we only

00:42:12.213 --> 00:42:13.917
have one container.

00:42:13.917 --> 00:42:15.000
So let's see what happens.

00:42:22.842 --> 00:42:23.550
And look at that.

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:25.341
The nodes have been
removed, deprovisioned,

00:42:25.341 --> 00:42:28.680
and went back to their
starting two-node cluster.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:32.790
So that is Kubernetes
Horizontal Pod Auto-scaling

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:36.690
using an RPS metric and
the cost-to-order scaler

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:39.834
to scale up and
down the cluster.

00:42:39.834 --> 00:42:40.625
Back to the slides.

00:42:43.810 --> 00:42:48.390
OK, so what did I show you?

00:42:48.390 --> 00:42:51.990
The recap is we looked at
readiness and liveness probes

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:55.980
and how important they were,
the importance of defining

00:42:55.980 --> 00:42:59.010
requesting limits so that
Kubernetes knows what resources

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:01.680
that you need, the idea
that you should be treating

00:43:01.680 --> 00:43:06.742
configuration as code using a
CI/CD pipeline to deploy code

00:43:06.742 --> 00:43:08.700
so that you're not going
to accidentally deploy

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:11.790
things that would just happen
to be sitting on your machine.

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:14.190
Using a CI system
means that you're only

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:16.200
going to deploy the code
that was checked in.

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:17.600
And with a CD
system, you're only

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:19.183
going to deploy the
configuration that

00:43:19.183 --> 00:43:21.130
was checked in.

00:43:21.130 --> 00:43:23.014
You should turn on
all the automation

00:43:23.014 --> 00:43:24.930
settings in Kubernetes
Engine, so you can just

00:43:24.930 --> 00:43:26.220
sleep easier at night--

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:28.840
and then finally,
auto-scale using RPS.

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:32.565
[MUSIC PLAYING]

